# stdlib.qgram Tutorial

`stdlib.qgram` provides padded character $q$-gram tokenization, overlap counting, Jaccard similarity, and an inverted index for fast fuzzy search prefiltering.

## Core API

- `qgrams(text: str, q: int) -> list`: Split `text` into overlapping $q$-grams padded with `\x01`
- `overlap(a: str, b: str, q: int) -> int`: Count shared $q$-grams between `a` and `b`
- `jaccard(a: str, b: str, q: int) -> float`: Jaccard similarity $|A \cap B| / |A \cup B|$ on $q$-gram sets
- `build_index(docs: list, q: int) -> dict`: Build an inverted index `qgram -> [doc_ids]`
- `query(index: dict, q_text: str, q: int) -> list`: Rank matching documents as `[doc_id, score]` pairs sorted descending

## Runnable Example

See `examples/qgram_tutorial.ltl` and `tests/stdlib_qgram_tutorial.ltl`:

```lateralus
import qgram

let grams = qgrams("cat", 2)
let ov = overlap("night", "nacht", 2)
let sim = jaccard("night", "nacht", 2)
let idx = build_index(["lateralus", "lathe", "banana"], 3)
let hits = query(idx, "late", 3)

assert(len(grams) == 4)
assert(ov == 3)
assert(sim > 0.33 && sim < 0.34)
assert(hits == [[0, 4], [1, 4]])
```

## Pinned Test Vectors

| Call | Expected Output |
|---|---|
| `qgrams("cat", 2)` | `["\x01c", "ca", "at", "t\x01"]` |
| `overlap("night", "nacht", 2)` | `3` |
| `jaccard("night", "nacht", 2)` | `0.333333...` (`1/3`) |
| `query(build_index(["lateralus", "lathe", "banana"], 3), "late", 3)` | `[[0, 4], [1, 4]]` |
